{"content_hash": "ac5e2ea46753b878", "version_id": 604, "article_id": 61, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Childhood cancer signs missed, parents told to act fast", "title_zh": "儿童癌症症状常被忽视，家长被敦促尽快行动", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "bbc_health", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwm237z77dgyo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1616408621507-88179d36a3a0?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8Mnx8Y2hpbGRob29kJTIwY2FuY2VyJTIwc3ltcHRvbXN8ZW58MHwwfHx8MTc5MTc2NTkwNnww&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Vitolda Klein on Unsplash", "author": "Vitolda Klein", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@little_klein?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/girl-in-white-and-blue-floral-dress-lying-on-white-fur-textile-ebiDpT4pYdw?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(195, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(233, 46%, 48%)", "label": "HEALTH", "seed": "2c5b225ed52f"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-12", "word_count": 245, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "Many parents do not feel confident about spotting childhood cancer symptoms, which can be mistaken for tiredness or hayfever. A new campaign urges families to look out for clusters of warning signs.", "lead_zh": "许多家长对识别儿童癌症症状缺乏信心，这些症状常被误认为疲劳或花粉症。一项新运动敦促家庭留意成组的警示信号。", "preview": "Although childhood cancer is rare, it is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK. About 1,900 children age", "preview_zh": "虽然儿童癌症很罕见，但它是英国15岁以下儿童因病死亡的主要原因。据英国癌症研究中心称，每年约有1900名0-14岁儿童被诊断出癌症。然而，只有不到三分之一的家长表示有信心识别这些症状。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 594, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/61/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 105.337, "size_bytes": 635904, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 287, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@f606c78/content/data/versions/604.json", "paragraphs": ["Although childhood cancer is rare, it is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK. About 1,900 children aged 0-14 are diagnosed each year, according to Cancer Research UK. Yet just under a third of parents say they feel confident recognising the symptoms.", "Thirteen-year-old Amelia Carroll was diagnosed with acute myeloid leukaemia last year. Her mother, Gemma, says the early signs were easy to overlook: tiredness and breathlessness after mild exercise were put down to being unfit. Only when Amelia developed bruises did Gemma realise something was wrong. Within 10 days, she had her diagnosis.", "Prof Bob Phillips, a paediatric oncologist, advises parents to watch for clusters of symptoms that persist or get worse. These include extreme tiredness, paleness, unexplained bruises, and lumps that grow over time. Cancer in children develops faster than in adults because they are growing. A national campaign promotes a rule-of-three: if a child has three persistent symptoms, three doctor visits, or three weeks of unexplained symptoms, consider a referral.", "Lynsey Swinscoe lost her son Jake to soft tissue cancer at 13. His early symptoms—a stuffy nose and watery eyes—were thought to be hayfever. Only a swelling on his nose led to a GP visit, where a mass was found. Jake had chemotherapy and radiation but chose to stop treatment. He died at home on 26 April 2024, two weeks before his 14th birthday. His mother now campaigns to raise awareness, calling it his legacy."], "paragraphs_zh": ["虽然儿童癌症很罕见，但它是英国15岁以下儿童因病死亡的主要原因。据英国癌症研究中心称，每年约有1900名0-14岁儿童被诊断出癌症。然而，只有不到三分之一的家长表示有信心识别这些症状。", "13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断出急性髓系白血病。她的母亲杰玛说，早期症状很容易被忽视：轻度运动后的疲倦和呼吸困难被归咎于体质差。直到阿米莉亚出现瘀伤，杰玛才意识到不对劲。10天内，她就得到了诊断。", "儿科肿瘤学家鲍勃·菲利普斯教授建议家长留意持续或恶化的成组症状。这些包括极度疲劳、面色苍白、不明原因的瘀伤，以及随着时间增大的肿块。儿童癌症比成人发展得更快，因为他们正在生长。一项全国性运动推广“三原则”：如果孩子有三种持续症状、看过三次医生，或有三周不明原因的症状，就应考虑转诊。", "林赛·斯温斯科失去了13岁的儿子杰克，他死于软组织癌。他的早期症状——鼻塞和流泪——被认为是花粉症。直到鼻梁出现肿胀才去看全科医生，发现了一个肿块。杰克接受了化疗和放疗，但选择停止治疗。他于2024年4月26日在家中去世，距离14岁生日还有两周。他的母亲现在致力于提高认识，称这是他的遗志。"], "body": "Although childhood cancer is rare, it is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK. About 1,900 children aged 0-14 are diagnosed each year, according to Cancer Research UK. Yet just under a third of parents say they feel confident recognising the symptoms.\n\nThirteen-year-old Amelia Carroll was diagnosed with acute myeloid leukaemia last year. Her mother, Gemma, says the early signs were easy to overlook: tiredness and breathlessness after mild exercise were put down to being unfit. Only when Amelia developed bruises did Gemma realise something was wrong. Within 10 days, she had her diagnosis.\n\nProf Bob Phillips, a paediatric oncologist, advises parents to watch for clusters of symptoms that persist or get worse. These include extreme tiredness, paleness, unexplained bruises, and lumps that grow over time. Cancer in children develops faster than in adults because they are growing. A national campaign promotes a rule-of-three: if a child has three persistent symptoms, three doctor visits, or three weeks of unexplained symptoms, consider a referral.\n\nLynsey Swinscoe lost her son Jake to soft tissue cancer at 13. His early symptoms—a stuffy nose and watery eyes—were thought to be hayfever. Only a swelling on his nose led to a GP visit, where a mass was found. Jake had chemotherapy and radiation but chose to stop treatment. He died at home on 26 April 2024, two weeks before his 14th birthday. His mother now campaigns to raise awareness, calling it his legacy.", "body_zh": "虽然儿童癌症很罕见，但它是英国15岁以下儿童因病死亡的主要原因。据英国癌症研究中心称，每年约有1900名0-14岁儿童被诊断出癌症。然而，只有不到三分之一的家长表示有信心识别这些症状。\n\n13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断出急性髓系白血病。她的母亲杰玛说，早期症状很容易被忽视：轻度运动后的疲倦和呼吸困难被归咎于体质差。直到阿米莉亚出现瘀伤，杰玛才意识到不对劲。10天内，她就得到了诊断。\n\n儿科肿瘤学家鲍勃·菲利普斯教授建议家长留意持续或恶化的成组症状。这些包括极度疲劳、面色苍白、不明原因的瘀伤，以及随着时间增大的肿块。儿童癌症比成人发展得更快，因为他们正在生长。一项全国性运动推广“三原则”：如果孩子有三种持续症状、看过三次医生，或有三周不明原因的症状，就应考虑转诊。\n\n林赛·斯温斯科失去了13岁的儿子杰克，他死于软组织癌。他的早期症状——鼻塞和流泪——被认为是花粉症。直到鼻梁出现肿胀才去看全科医生，发现了一个肿块。杰克接受了化疗和放疗，但选择停止治疗。他于2024年4月26日在家中去世，距离14岁生日还有两周。他的母亲现在致力于提高认识，称这是他的遗志。", "text": "Although childhood cancer is rare, it is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK. About 1,900 children aged 0-14 are diagnosed each year, according to Cancer Research UK. Yet just under a third of parents say they feel confident recognising the symptoms. Thirteen-year-old Amelia Carroll was diagnosed with acute myeloid leukaemia last year. Her mother, Gemma, says the early signs were easy to overlook: tiredness and breathlessness after mild exercise were put down to being unfit. Only when Amelia developed bruises did Gemma realise something was wrong. Within 10 days, she had her diagnosis. Prof Bob Phillips, a paediatric oncologist, advises parents to watch for clusters of symptoms that persist or get worse. These include extreme tiredness, paleness, unexplained bruises, and lumps that grow over time. Cancer in children develops faster than in adults because they are growing. A national campaign promotes a rule-of-three: if a child has three persistent symptoms, three doctor visits, or three weeks of unexplained symptoms, consider a referral. Lynsey Swinscoe lost her son Jake to soft tissue cancer at 13. His early symptoms—a stuffy nose and watery eyes—were thought to be hayfever. Only a swelling on his nose led to a GP visit, where a mass was found. Jake had chemotherapy and radiation but chose to stop treatment. He died at home on 26 April 2024, two weeks before his 14th birthday. His mother now campaigns to raise awareness, calling it his legacy.", "vocab": [{"word": "diagnosed", "pos": "verb", "zh": "被诊断", "note": "常用被动语态：be diagnosed with", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "symptom", "pos": "noun", "zh": "症状", "note": "可数名词，常与 of 连用", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "leukaemia", "pos": "noun", "zh": "白血病", "note": "血液癌症的一种", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "persist", "pos": "verb", "zh": "持续", "note": "指症状持续不退", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "referral", "pos": "noun", "zh": "转诊", "note": "医生将病人转给专科医生", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "oncologist", "pos": "noun", "zh": "肿瘤学家", "note": "专门治疗癌症的医生", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "cluster", "pos": "noun", "zh": "一组，一群", "note": "常指症状成组出现", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "unexplained", "pos": "adj", "zh": "无法解释的", "note": "常修饰症状或瘀伤", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "remission", "pos": "noun", "zh": "缓解期", "note": "癌症症状减轻或消失的时期", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "awareness", "pos": "noun", "zh": "意识，认识", "note": "raise awareness 提高认识", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "legacy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "遗志，遗产", "note": "指某人留下的精神财富", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "survival rate", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "存活率", "note": "指患者存活的比例", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for", "summary_original": "Although rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK.", "body_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for\nBy\nCamilla Horrox\nHealth reporter\nPublished\n1 hour ago\nAlthough rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK - yet just under a third of parents say they feel confident recognising the symptoms.\nAbout 1,900 children aged 0-14 are diagnosed with cancer each year, according to Cancer Research UK.\nThirteen-year-old Amelia Carroll, who is now in remission, was diagnosed with acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) last year.\nHer mum Gemma recalls the initial symptoms being easy to overlook.\nThere was the tiredness, she says, and being out of breath after mild exercise but both were put down to being unfit and just post-school fatigue.\nIt was only when Amelia started presenting with bruises Gemma knew something wasn't right - within 10 days Amelia got her leukaemia diagnosis.\nWhat are the symptoms to look out for?\nProf Bob Phillips, a paediatric oncologist, says it's key for parents to look out for clusters of symptoms that may be persisting, getting worse or bigger.\nConsider contacting a doctor over the following:\nExtreme tiredness\nLooking really pale\nBruises that you can't explain\nLumps and bumps that are getting bigger or not going away and growing over time\nCancer in children develops much more quickly than in adults - effectively due to them growing.\nExperts like Phillips at CCLG, the Children and Young People's Cancer Association, are urging people to follow a national campaign aiming to raise awareness and improve diagnosis times.\nIt includes a rule-of-three for professionals: If a child has had three persistent symptoms, or has visited their doctor three times, or has had three weeks of unexplained symptoms consider making a referral.\nAlthough, it's important to note survival rates have risen significantly since the 1970s, with eight out of 10 children surviving beyond five to 10 years or more after diagnosis.\nFor Amelia, her cancer journey began with a single red bruise that came out of nowhere, says Gemma.\nThree days later, she had bruises down both of her shins on both legs, which were sore and raised.\n\"We'd gone to the opticians for a routine appointment and they noticed that she'd got some burst blood vessels but they weren't sure why,\" she adds.\nWithin 10 days, doctors had confirmed what type of leukaemia Amelia had and she was admitted to hospital to begin treatment.\n\"On the day that they confirmed what she had, I sat her down and she said to me 'have I got cancer?' and I said yes you have. She asked me if she was going to die.\"\n\"I said no, they're doing everything they can, they've got a plan in place and the treatment is going to do everything it can to beat the cancer.\"\nAmelia is currently in remission, undergoing regular bone marrow and blood tests every three months, and has returned to school.\nPhillips says survival rates for childhood cancer in the UK are over 80% but that even quicker diagnosis would lead to less intensive treatments.\n'Raising awareness is his legacy'\nAround the UK, about half the children diagnosed with cancer get their diagnosis within two weeks, he says.\n\"It's pretty quick but we want the other half to not have the wait of 16, 18, 20, 25 weeks from those first symptoms to get a diagnosis.\"\nCancer Research UK data shows that there are more than 400,000 new cases of cancer diagnosed every year in the UK, with only 54% being told within the 28-day NHS target window, according to the British Medical Journal (BMJ).\nAn additional 2,300 teenagers and young adults (aged 15–24) are diagnosed each year.\nLynsey Swinscoe is mum to Jake, who died of soft tissue cancer aged 13. Jake was diagnosed with stage three, fusion positive alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma in July 2023.\nBefore his diagnosis, Jake's initial symptoms of a stuffy nose and watery eyes were put down to hayfever, and falling asleep after school was thought to be just end-of-term tiredness.\n\"It was only when he started to get a swelling across the bridge of his nose that we thought right, we need to take him to the GP,\" Lynsey says.\n\"But at that point we didn't really think the fatigue and hayfever symptoms were related.\"\nA mass was found in Jake's nose and nasal cavity and he was diagnosed with soft tissue sarcoma. He completed nine rounds of chemotherapy and proton beam radiation.\nAfter suffering seizures and feeling very unwell from the chemotherapy, Jake opted out of continuing treatment.\nHe passed away peacefully at home on 26 April 2024, just two weeks before his 14th birthday.\n\"I think the main thing with Jake is he was not scared to die. We had lots of conversations about dying and what it might feel like to die and the only thing that he was sort of scared about was, 'are all the people going to remember me? Is everyone just going to forget who I am?'\n\"That's why I'm doing this, to make sure that people know who Jake is, to make sure people know how incredible he was. And so with that comes the raising awareness, which I think, that's his legacy.\"\nRelated topics\nLeukaemia\nCancer\nChild health\nNHS\nHealth\nChildhood cancer\nMore on this story\nMother writes book to help children with cancer\nPublished\n4 October\nGirl, 7, rings bell to mark end of cancer treatment\nPublished\n1 October\n'This doesn't happen to us' - mum on child's cancer\nPublished\n12 September", "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T00:45:05.386127", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Although", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.45, "sm": 100, "em": 449, "cs": 0, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "childhood", "s": 0.463, "e": 0.975, "sm": 462, "em": 975, "cs": 9, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "cancer", "s": 0.988, "e": 1.413, "sm": 987, "em": 1412, "cs": 19, "ce": 25, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "is", "s": 1.425, "e": 1.55, "sm": 1425, "em": 1550, "cs": 26, "ce": 28, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "rare", "s": 1.562, "e": 1.938, "sm": 1562, "em": 1937, "cs": 29, "ce": 33, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": ",", "s": 1.938, "e": 2.25, "sm": 1937, "em": 2250, "cs": 33, "ce": 34, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "it", "s": 2.25, "e": 2.325, "sm": 2250, "em": 2325, "cs": 35, "ce": 37, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "is", "s": 2.337, "e": 2.438, "sm": 2337, "em": 2437, "cs": 38, "ce": 40, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "the", "s": 2.45, "e": 2.55, "sm": 2450, "em": 2550, "cs": 41, "ce": 44, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "leading", "s": 2.562, "e": 2.888, "sm": 2562, "em": 2887, "cs": 45, "ce": 52, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "cause", "s": 2.9, "e": 3.225, "sm": 2900, "em": 3225, "cs": 53, "ce": 58, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "of", "s": 3.237, "e": 3.325, "sm": 3237, "em": 3324, "cs": 59, "ce": 61, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "death", "s": 3.337, "e": 3.65, "sm": 3337, "em": 3650, "cs": 62, "ce": 67, "s